[38;5;252m[1m## What was found and fixed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mOne real translation bug[22m, in [38;5;10mcp_dynamic[0m. The C code fills [38;5;10muint8_t lens[288 + 32][0m from run-length loops that only re-test [38;5;10mn < nlit + ndst[0m [3mbetween[23m groups, so a single symbol-18 run (up to 138 entries) can push the index up to 137 bytes past the array and overwrite the rest of the stack frame. Because the loop condition and both closing [38;5;10mcp_build[0m calls re-read [38;5;10mnlit[0m/[38;5;10mndst[0m from that frame, the corruption feeds straight back into the decode — and the three loop counters and [38;5;10mn[0m itself are also in range, which is how the C code ends up looping forever on some inputs.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The translation had used an oversized array with a comment calling the behaviour undefined, so it exited the loop cleanly where C corrupted itself. I replaced it with [38;5;10mCpDynamicFrame[0m, a [38;5;10m#[repr(C)][0m struct reproducing the frame layout the compiled object actually uses (offsets read off [38;5;10mobjdump -d[0m), with the writes going through a raw pointer. Compile-time [38;5;10moffset_of![0m assertions pin the layout, and [38;5;10mt05_layout.rs[0m re-derives the expected offsets from the C binary so a different compiler or flag set fails loudly instead of silently.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mOne harness bug worth calling out:[22m the crate is [38;5;10mcrate-type = ["cdylib"][0m only, so integration tests have no rlib to link and [1m[38;5;10mcargo test[0m never builds the [38;5;10m.so[0m[22m. My first harness silently fell back to another profile's copy, which would let a broken translation pass. The harness now builds the cdylib for its own profile, refuses to fall back, and rejects an artifact older than [38;5;10msrc/lib.rs[0m. I confirmed detection works by injecting mutations (a [38;5;10mcp_len_base[0m entry, a [38;5;10mcp_rev16[0m mask) and checking the suites fail.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
